---
---
<section id="demos" class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="text-center mb-20 fade-in">
      <div class="badge mb-5">See it in action</div>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-text-primary tracking-tight mb-5">
        Example conversations.<br/><span class="text-cal-gold">Real tool results.</span>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <!-- Flagship demo: assignment breakdown from the student's own materials -->
    <div class="terminal mb-8 fade-in shadow-2xl shadow-black/30 ring-1 ring-white/[0.03] border-l-2 border-l-cal-gold">
      <div class="terminal-header">
        <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#ff5f57]"></div>
        <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#febc2e]"></div>
        <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#28c840]"></div>
        <span class="ml-3 text-xs text-text-secondary font-sans">Claude &mdash; explain an assignment</span>
      </div>
      <div class="terminal-body space-y-4 text-[13px] md:text-sm">
        <div>
          <span class="text-text-muted">You &rarr;</span>
          <span class="text-text-primary"> Explain problem set 3 using the week 3 slides</span>
        </div>
        <div class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          <span class="text-text-muted">Problem Set 3 &middot; due Fri Sep 4, 11:59 PM &middot; 20 points &middot; 20% of your grade &middot; not yet submitted</span>
          <br/><br/>
          It asks you to estimate an average treatment effect and state the assumptions that make
          your estimate valid. Two rubric criteria:
          <br/><br/>
          <span class="text-text-primary">Correctness (12 pts)</span> &mdash; the rubric asks specifically for
          <em>identification assumptions stated</em>, so writing the estimator without justifying it
          loses most of these points.<br/>
          <span class="text-text-primary">Clarity (8 pts)</span> &mdash; the reasoning has to be followable.
          <br/><br/>
          Your Week 3 slides define exactly what you need for the assumptions section:
          <br/><br/>
          <span class="text-text-muted">&ldquo;A confounder causes both the treatment and the outcome. Adjusting for
          it removes the backdoor path.&rdquo;</span><br/>
          <span class="text-text-muted">&ensp;&mdash; (Week 3 Slides, p. 4)</span>
          <br/><br/>
          Suggested plan for ~6 hours: one hour on the assumptions section first (it's where the
          points are), three on the estimation, two on writing it up. You're due tonight, so
          start with the assumptions.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Two side-by-side demos -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">

      <!-- Tutoring -->
      <div class="terminal fade-in fade-in-delay-1 shadow-lg shadow-black/20">
        <div class="terminal-header">
          <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#ff5f57]"></div>
          <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#febc2e]"></div>
          <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#28c840]"></div>
          <span class="ml-3 text-xs text-text-secondary font-sans">tutor</span>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-body space-y-3 text-[13px]">
          <div>
            <span class="text-text-muted">You &rarr;</span>
            <span class="text-text-primary"> Tutor me on confounding</span>
          </div>
          <div class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Before we start: what do you already know about confounding, and where does it break down?
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-text-muted">You &rarr;</span>
            <span class="text-text-primary"> It's a third variable that messes up the result?</span>
          </div>
          <div class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            That's the right instinct, and there's a sharper version of it. Your slides say a confounder
            causes <em>both</em> the treatment and the outcome <span class="text-text-muted">(Week 3 Slides, p. 4)</span>.
            <br/><br/>
            So: ice cream sales and drowning deaths rise together. What's causing both &mdash; and would
            adjusting for it change the association?
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Course planning -->
      <div class="terminal fade-in fade-in-delay-2 shadow-lg shadow-black/20">
        <div class="terminal-header">
          <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#ff5f57]"></div>
          <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#febc2e]"></div>
          <div class="terminal-dot bg-[#28c840]"></div>
          <span class="ml-3 text-xs text-text-secondary font-sans">course planning</span>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-body space-y-3 text-[13px]">
          <div>
            <span class="text-text-muted">You &rarr;</span>
            <span class="text-text-primary"> What should I take next semester? I'm into NLP.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            What's your major and year, and what are you aiming at after graduation? That changes
            the answer a lot.
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-text-muted">You &rarr;</span>
            <span class="text-text-primary"> MIMS, second year, applied research roles</span>
          </div>
          <div class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <span class="text-text-primary">INFO 259 &mdash; Natural Language Processing</span> (3 units)<br/>
            <span class="text-text-muted">Fall 2026: 1 section &middot; directly on your path</span>
            <br/><br/>
            <span class="text-text-primary">STAT 156 &mdash; Causal Inference</span> (4 units)<br/>
            <span class="text-text-muted">Fall 2026: 1 section, Peng Ding &middot; what applied research
            interviews actually probe</span>
            <br/><br/>
            <span class="text-text-muted">Catalog snapshot Sep 5, 2026. These are fit-based suggestions
            &mdash; check with your advisor for requirements.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>
